course: everyday
lesson: 283
title: Staying safe online and checking what you see
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Online, things are not always what they look like. A fake site can copy a real one with one changed letter («amaz0n» instead of «amazon»), a «free» game from an unofficial site may carry a virus, and a stranger's friend request can come from anyone pretending to be anyone.
explain: Pictures and videos can be fake too. Photos get edited, AI can make images with small mistakes like extra fingers or blurry writing, and deepfake videos can make famous people seem to say things they never said. Old stories are often reshared as if they were new.
explain: A good checker asks: Who wrote this? Where do the facts come from? What is the date? Do trusted news sources say the same? Headlines in big capitals and posts saying «share this before it gets deleted!» are built to make you click and spread, not to make you think.
explain: Example: after a hurricane, a photo shows a shark swimming on a flooded highway in Houston. It looks real, but a reverse image search shows it was edited from an old picture. This exact fake has been shared after many storms.
explain: Mistakes happen. If you click a scam link, tell a parent straight away; you will not be in trouble for being honest, and changing passwords quickly can stop the damage.
fact: Ask a parent before allowing app permissions, and only allow what the app really needs; apps can collect and sell your information.
fact: Fake websites copy real ones with tiny spelling changes, so check the address carefully.
fact: Do not download paid games for free from unofficial sites; they often carry viruses or steal information.
fact: Only accept friend requests from people you know in real life, because strangers can pretend to be anyone.
fact: If you click a scam link by mistake, tell a parent at once; changing passwords quickly can stop the damage.
fact: Real police do not ask for payment by message or phone; «pay or be arrested» messages are scare scams.
fact: Headlines are written to make you click, so read beyond them and check other sources.
fact: «Share this before it gets deleted!» is a trick to make false stories spread fast.
fact: Edited and fake photos often spread after big events; an adult can help with a reverse image search.
fact: AI images can look real but often have small mistakes, like extra fingers or blurry writing.
fact: Videos and voices can be faked (deepfakes), so check trusted news sources.
fact: A fact can be checked and proved, like the Earth going around the Sun; an opinion is what someone thinks or likes, like which season is best.
fact: Check the date, because old stories are often reshared as if they just happened.
fact: Satire sites like The Onion write made-up news as jokes; it is not lying when readers know, but it can fool people who don't.
fact: Trust sites that say who wrote them and where their facts come from; scientists have shown in many ways, including photos from space, that the Earth is round.
exercise: A new drawing app asks to use your contacts, camera and location. What is right? | Ask a parent and only allow what the app really needs | Allow everything so the app works best and never crashes | Allow everything but turn it off later | Delete your contacts before installing
exercise: Why should apps not get every permission they ask for? | Apps can collect and sell your information | Permissions make the phone's screen go darker | Apps stop working properly if you allow them to do things | Permissions cost money each time you allow one
exercise: A link takes you to «paypa1.com» which looks just like PayPal. What is going on? | It is probably a fake site with a tiny spelling change | It is PayPal's special site for children, so it is safe to use | It is PayPal's new address | Spelling doesn't matter in web addresses
exercise: A website offers a paid game for free download. What should you do? | Don't download it; unofficial sites often carry viruses | Download it quickly before somebody takes it down for good | Download it on a parent's computer instead | Share the link with friends first
exercise: Someone you've never met sends you a friend request with a cute dog photo. What do you do? | Only accept people you know in real life | Accept, because the dog looks friendly and they might be fun | Accept and ask where they live | Accept if they say they are your age
exercise: Oops, you clicked a strange link that asked for your password. What now? | Tell a parent straight away so passwords can be changed | Hide it so you don't get in trouble, and hope nothing happens | Click it again to undo it | Wait to see if anything happens
exercise: A message says «Pay $200 now or the police will arrest you». What is it? | A scare scam; real police do not ask for payment like that | A real warning from the police, so you should pay it at once | A joke from your friends | A bill from your school
exercise: A headline screams «SCIENTISTS SHOCKED BY KILLER BANANAS!». What should you do? | Read beyond the headline and check other sources | Throw away all your bananas | Share it before anyone else | Believe it, because it's written in capitals on a news website
exercise: A post says «Share this before it gets deleted!». How should you treat it? | Be suspicious and do not share it | Share it fast to save the truth before they hide it | Share it only with your close friends | Copy it quickly in case it vanishes
exercise: A photo shows a shark swimming on a flooded motorway. What is a good way to check it? | Ask an adult to help with a reverse image search | Believe it, because photos can't lie and someone took it there | Count the shark's teeth | Ask whoever posted it if it is real
exercise: A picture of a «real» person has six fingers on one hand and blurry writing behind. What might it be? | An image made by AI | A rare photo of a real person | A photo taken in the dark | A painting from a museum
exercise: A video shows the Prime Minister saying something very strange. What is wise? | Remember videos can be faked and check trusted news | Believe it, because it's a video and you can see him say it | Share it to warn people | Assume he really said it
exercise: A story about a big storm is shared as «breaking news», but something seems off. What should you check first? | The date it was published | How many likes it has | The colour of the website | Whether it has a picture
exercise: Your friend shares a story from The Onion as if it were real. What is The Onion? | A satire site that writes made-up news as jokes | A trusted science magazine | A real newspaper from London that has printed news for years | A cooking website about onions
exercise: For homework you find a fact on a website with no author and no sources. What is wise? | Prefer sites that say who wrote them and where facts come from | Use it, because anything that's online has been checked by someone | Use it if it has nice pictures | Copy it exactly to be safe
exercise: A confident video says the Earth is flat. What is true? | Scientists have shown in many ways, including from space, that it is round | It must be true because he sounds sure | Nobody really knows the true shape of the Earth, even from space | Only old books say the Earth is round
test: A torch app asks for your contacts and location. Why is that suspicious? | A torch does not need contacts or location to work | All torches need maps and phone numbers to shine properly | It is normal for every app | It makes the light brighter
test: You see «www.roblocks.com» in an email about your Roblox account. What should you notice? | The name is misspelled, so it may be a fake site | It is Roblox's secret site that only special players get to see | It is fine, close enough | It must be Roblox's newer site
test: A boy in Leeds clicked a «free skins» link and then told his mum, though he was afraid. Why was telling her right? | Acting fast, like changing passwords, can stop the damage | Telling her undoes the click by itself and makes the link go away | His mum will buy him the skins | It was not right; he should hide it
test: A post shows a tornado photo with the caption «Happening RIGHT NOW in Kansas!». It was actually taken in 2013. What happened? | An old picture was reshared as if new | Tornadoes repeat the same way each year | The date on the camera had been set wrong | It must be a brand new tornado this year
test: Which is a FACT, not an opinion? | The Earth goes around the Sun | Summer is the best season of all | Cats are much nicer pets than dogs | Football is the most boring sport
test: A video shows a famous singer saying she hates her fans, but no news site reports it. What is most likely? | It could be a deepfake, so wait for trusted news | She really said it in secret and it got leaked | All news sites missed it | Videos are always real
test: An online stranger offers to be your friend and says he is 10, like you. Why be careful? | Online, anyone can pretend to be anyone | All 10-year-olds online are safe to talk to | Strangers online never lie about their age | It is only a problem if he is rude to you
test: A friend says «It's on the internet so it's true». Which answer is best? | Anyone can write anything online, so check sources | Yes, websites are checked by the government | Yes, if it has a photo | No, nothing at all that's on the internet is ever true
test: A satire article says a school banned homework forever, and your little brother believes it. What should you explain? | It is a joke article, not real news | It is real news from that very school | Satire is just a sneaky type of lying | All articles with jokes are fake news
test: game app wants my contacts n location, just say yes? | Ask a parent and only allow what it really needs | Yes, apps need everything to work, just tap allow | Yes, nobody uses that information | Only say no to the camera
test: oops clicked a weird link, what now | Tell a parent right away, change passwords quick | Do nothing, it'll go away | Click it again to close it | Delete the whole app and forget it ever happened
test: pic of a shark on the motorway after the flood, real? | Maybe fake, such pics spread after big events, check it | Yes, it's a photo so it must be real, cameras don't lie | Yes, sharks swim in floods often | No, all flood photos are fake
test: this AI pic of the queen looks so real tho | Look for slips like extra fingers or blurry writing | AI pictures are always perfect, you can never spot one | If it looks real, it is real | Only black and white pics are fake
explain: A boy in Canada downloaded a «free» copy of a paid game from a strange site; it put a virus on the family laptop and his dad's passwords were stolen.
explain: A girl in Scotland accepted a friend request from a «girl her age» she did not know; it was really a grown-up who kept asking for photos, and her parents had to call the police.
fact: People online are not always who they say they are, and some adults pretend to be children to trick them.
fact: Free copies of paid games on unofficial sites often carry viruses that steal passwords.
test: random account says shes 10 and wants to be friends | Only accept people you know in real life | Accept, she's the same age | Accept if her picture looks nice | Accept and send her your number so you can chat more
test: clicked a dodgy link by accident, am i in trouble | Tell a parent now so passwords can be changed quickly | Keep quiet and hope it's fine, nobody will ever know | Click it again to undo it | Delete the browser and say nothing
test: message says 'police: pay £50 or be arrested' | Tell a parent; real police never ask for payment like that | Pay quickly so you are not arrested, then tell nobody | Reply and ask for more time | Send your address to explain
test: did u hear a boy got a free copy of a new game off some random site and "his whole laptop got hacked, even his mum's bank". lesson? | Don't download paid games free from unofficial sites | Free game sites are fine as long as they look good and safe | Only download at night when it's quiet | Just turn the laptop off after
test: someone posted a video of a famous footballer saying the match is fixed, everyone's sharing it "cos it's real, you can hear him". what's likely | It may be a deepfake; check trusted news first | It must be true because you can hear his voice in it | Videos can never be faked | Lots of shares prove it's real
test: kids are saying the new boy "is really 30 pretending to be 11" cos he's tall. spread it? | No, it's a cruel rumour with nothing behind it | Yes, everyone should be warned | Yes, and post his photo with it so everyone knows who it is | Yes, but only to your best friend
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which fraudulent websites deceive their visitors? | They copy genuine sites with minute alterations in spelling of the address | They are invariably written in a foreign language and poor grammar | They display no logos whatsoever | They are accessible only at night
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the date of a news story is to be verified before it is shared. | Old stories are frequently reshared as though they had just occurred | Stories older than one week are illegal to share under the law | The date reveals the name of the author | Recent stories are invariably false
exam: In respect of images generated by artificial intelligence, select the response consistent with the established facts. | They may appear real yet often contain small errors such as extra fingers | They are always clearly labelled as artificial by the law | They cannot depict human beings | They are invariably in black and white
exam: It is given that a child has clicked a fraudulent link in error. State the action most consistent with limiting the damage. | The child shall inform a parent at once so that passwords may be changed | The child shall conceal the matter to avoid any blame or punishment | The child shall click the link again to undo it | The child shall delete every application on the device
